From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm cuda: FP8 latent keys fetched a tile ahead, in 8-byte loads

Our fork `ai-engine`, ref `fork-ssh/ovn-up`, commit 450b9519c "glm cuda: FP8
latent keys fetched a tile ahead, in 8-byte loads", taken for the slab-only
half of chunks_wide.

The pin's FP8 arm of glm_attn::chunks_wide converts a 32-key tile right after
the tile before was computed, with eight single-byte global loads a slot. A
block of these kernels holds about 100 KB of shared memory, so it is alone on
its SM and every gather's latency is exposed, while the BF16 arm hides its
loads behind cp.async. This splits the FP8 work in two: fetch(tile) reads each
of a thread's slots as one 8-byte uint2 plus its row scale into registers, and
store(tile) converts from those registers and writes 16 bytes at a time into
the tile's shared buffer. The entry does fetch(0), store(0), fetch(1); the tile
loop, just after its barrier, stores tile + 1 into the buffer the barrier freed
and fetches tile + 2. The BF16 arm keeps its cp.async pair, so load_kv is now
BF16 only and its commit at the loop tail is guarded by !FP8.

This completes 0021-mla-knobs. That patch brought zig/kernels/cuda/
glm_glue_attn_ovn.cuh from the fork, and that file's chunks<HB, FP8, FUSED>
already carries this exact fetch/store pair (its lines 45 to 85 and 111 to 136),
so until now the skip and fused variants fetched a tile ahead while the default
tf_glm_chunks_16_fp8 and tf_glm_chunks_32_fp8 did not. The pin owns chunks_wide
itself, which is why no earlier patch in the stack touches these lines.

The bits are unchanged, expression by expression:

- byte extraction. The pin builds `bits` for even j as codes[j] | codes[j+1]<<8
  over the slot's eight bytes. The uint2 load reads the same eight bytes, x the
  first four and y the last four, little-endian on every CUDA device, so the
  four code pairs are x & 0xffff, x >> 16, y & 0xffff, y >> 16 in that order,
  which is the pin's j = 0, 2, 4, 6. The load is 8-byte aligned: codes is the
  cache base plus src * LW with LW 512, and u * 8 is a multiple of 8.
- the dead-key case. The pin takes bits 0 and row_scale 1.0 when !ok; the fetch
  takes pre_c zero and pre_s 1.0, so every pair converts from 0 as before and
  the slot is still written.
- conversion and rounding. __nv_cvt_fp8x2_to_halfraw2, __half2, __half22float2
  and __fmul_rn are the pin's calls with the pin's operands.
  __floats2bfloat162_rn(a, b) is __float2bfloat16_rn of each half packed as one
  word, the same round to nearest even the pin's two scalar conversions use.
- placement. wide(key, u) is a multiple of 8 elements and the shared base is
  16-byte aligned, so one uint4 covers exactly the pin's dst[wide(key,u)+0..7],
  in the same element order: out[j] holds pair j as (low half, high half).
- coverage. The pin's strided loop runs idx = t, t + blockDim.x, ... below
  KT * (LW / 8) = 2048. All four wrappers are __launch_bounds__(256) and the
  host launches 256 threads, so PER is 8 and idx = t + p * 256 is the same set.
- ordering. A tile's buffer is (tile & 1); store(tile + 1) writes the other
  buffer, whose last reader was tile - 1, and two __syncthreads() separate them.

Those were swept rather than argued, in two passes, because most of this can be
settled without a device:

- the plumbing, with the conversion stood in for by a marker that keeps the
  input and the half it came from, over every value of every code pair at four
  slot positions in the row: 1,048,576 cases, 0 differ.
- the whole conversion, host side with the real intrinsics
  (__nv_cvt_fp8x2_to_halfraw2, __half2, __half22float2, __float2bfloat16_rn and
  __floats2bfloat162_rn, built by nvcc 13.3 with the build's own defines), both
  forms written out as the two files write them, over every value of every code
  pair at nine row scales including 0, 1, a subnormal-making 3.05e-05 and
  65504: 2,359,296 cases, 0 differ. This is the pass that settles the fourth
  point, that one __floats2bfloat162_rn equals the pin's two scalar
  __float2bfloat16_rn conversions, which was prose before.

Neither sweep is vacuous: a mutant that swaps a pair's two halves is caught by
both. What they cannot speak to is the device's own rounding of the multiply
(the host path uses the same operation on both sides, so the comparison holds
but __fmul_rn's SASS is not exercised) and the scheduling, which is the fifth
and sixth points above and needs a GPU.

Left out of the fork's hunks: the pt and pstep page addressing. The fork's
fetch resolves a part page and reads the scales from past the page's codes when
pt is set. FP8 on pages is an unported feature here, refused by cuda_start.zig
and cuda_latent.zig, so chunks_wide keeps its slab-only addressing and the one
comment line about it says so. Our line keeps `scales[src]` where the fork
hoists `const float* row_scales = scales + src` to carry the page case, the
same value. The fork's two-line comment above PER is one line here, with the
256-thread half moved onto PER's own line, because this repo's lean gate takes
a comment on one line. Nothing else in the commit's five hunks is dropped.

Compiled here, before and after, with the build's own flags: nvcc 13.3 (the
Windows toolkit on this workstation), torch_flags plus -O3 and the Windows
preprocessor pair cuda_fatbin.zig passes, -I zig/kernels/cuda, one -gencode a
recorded SM. Both sides build glm_glue clean at sm_80, 86, 89, 120 and 121.
That is a compile, not a numerical check: the bytes still need a device.

What ptxas reports, registers a thread, in the record's SM order
(80, 86, 89, 120, 121):

  tf_glm_chunks_16_fp8   64, 64, 64, 64, 64      ->  148, 148, 150, 128, 128
  tf_glm_chunks_32_fp8   128, 128, 127, 130, 130 ->  194, 194, 213, 163, 163
  tf_glm_chunks_16       64, 64, 64, 123, 123    ->  unchanged
  tf_glm_chunks_32       150, 150, 150, 128, 128 ->  unchanged

Neither changed kernel spills a byte on any of the five, before or after. The
two kernels that do spill in this image, tf_glm_chunks_16_fp8_skip at 24 bytes
and tf_glm_select_rows_wide at 8, spill the same before and after, so neither
is this patch. The BF16 rows confirm the fork's claim that its arm is untouched.

The before column reproduces what measurements/pending_133.zig already records
for both kernels, exactly, which is what makes the after column substitutable:
the instrument agrees with the receipt it is replacing. So this patch updates
that file, the nvcc 13.3 receipts, with the measured numbers.

It does not touch glm_kv_pending.zig. That file says nvcc 13.0, and 13.0 does
not build this tree on this workstation: CUDA 13.0 and 13.1 predate glibc
2.43's C23 rsqrt, so their crt/math_functions.h collides with the system
mathcalls.h before any of our code is reached. Five ways round it were tried
and none works: plain, -Xcompiler -fpermissive, neutering __THROW, and two
diag_suppress forms (the tag is rejected and the front end emits no error
number for this one, because an exception-specification mismatch is
ill-formed rather than a suppressible diagnostic). Writing the 13.3
measurement into a file labelled 13.0 would be a false provenance, so its two
rows are stale after this patch and the build machine has to retake them.

Which releases that actually affects, read out of registry.usageFor rather than
assumed. newer(x, y) is strictly x after y, so the later_pending line keeps a
record's usage while the release is below 13.2 and zeroes it from 13.2 on.
Neither kernel is in measurements/main_130.zig, which usageFor consults first at
exactly 13.0. So for these two: 13.0 and 13.1 read glm_kv_pending's numbers,
which are the stale ones; 13.2 falls through pending_132.lookup and finds
nothing, so none; 13.3 takes measurements/pending_133.zig, the file this patch
updates; 13.4 and later find nothing either and are none. The stale pair is read
on exactly the two compilers this box cannot run, and not on any later one, so
the hole is narrow but it is not where an earlier draft of this header put it.
The fork leaves both files untouched in this commit and still reads 64 and
128/127/130 at ovn-up.

What a GPU run has to show. Byte equality: tf_glm_chunks_16_fp8 and
tf_glm_chunks_32_fp8 against the same kernels before this patch, dense and
sparse, at counts that straddle the tile and chunk edges, including a count of
zero and counts that leave a tile partly dead. Then the time: the fork claims
this stage is about 220 ms of a 2,048-row prompt chunk, so the prompt side is
where the win should appear, with decode's chunks_16_fp8 a smaller share. One
thing to watch beside the clock: 128 registers a thread at 256 threads is two
blocks an SM worth of registers, but these kernels already take one block an SM
on shared memory, so the register rise should not move occupancy. Confirm that
rather than assume it.

Re-seated on v1.2.7 (93a2e93ecebff8aa52b88e58c5dd222516e69c5f, from pin 9c9bf542) by the 2026-11-12 re-seat (work/reseat/DECISIONS.md):
- 42735f94f moved glm_attn::chunks_wide from glm_glue_attn.cuh into zig/kernels/cuda/common/mla.cuh (shared with the tf_mla_* entries) and gave it RW and SPLIT template parameters. The fold's FP8 fetch/store split is merged into that function (git merge-file of the function's three texts): load_kv keeps v1.2.7's KW-wide BF16 copy, fetch and store are the fold's (LW wide; static_assert(!(FP8 && RW != 0)) makes KW == LW on the FP8 arm).
- measurements/pending_133.zig carries the fold's after-registers for tf_glm_chunks_16_fp8 and _32_fp8, now re-measured on this body: nvcc 13.3 ptxas (the build's flags, work/reseat/nvcc_check.py) reports 148, 148, 150, 128, 128 and 194, 194, 213, 163, 163 at sm_80/86/89/120/121, the file's numbers exactly, against v1.2.7's 64 x5 and 128, 128, 127, 130, 130. Neither kernel spills on any of the five (spill_check.py).
- source: work/fold/0031-fp8-tile-ahead.patch

